From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jaime Dias <jaimejosediasnt@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 6 Aug 2026 18:00:00 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 5/5] Answer unmapped guest addresses with open bus, not a fault

An access that reaches the fall-through in SYSWriteValToAddr() or
SYSReadValFromAddr() is emulated by touching the linear alias of the guest
address space. When the guest address is one the machine does not have --
a pointer went wild, or software is sizing memory -- that alias is not
mapped, so the access faults *inside the fault handler*, which re-enters
itself. 512 KB of ARM stack is gone in a few thousand turns and the machine
hangs with its PC parked on the exception vector.

Measured, not hypothetical: three stalls out of three in one series, all
first faulting on a guest write, once to 0x64656275 -- the bytes "debu",
a pointer to the string "debug" being used as an address.

A real 68k board answers an unclaimed address with open bus. Do that:
swallow the write, read back all ones, and say so on the console with the
report count capped so a memory-sizing sweep cannot become the bottleneck.

Whether an address is backed is asked of the MMU, not of a table of regions.
`at s1e1r`/`s1e1w` puts the address through the same stage-1 translation the
load or store would use and reports in PAR_EL1. That cannot disagree with
reality, which a region list can and did: an earlier attempt at this guard
rejected everything outside sys_memory above 16 MB, which included the
framebuffer at 0x3c100000 -- the machine booted perfectly and nothing could
ever appear on screen.

This does not fix the guest going wild. It makes that failure survivable and
reportable instead of destroying the emulator that would have to diagnose it.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
--- a/src/aarch64/vectors.c
+++ b/src/aarch64/vectors.c
@@ -317,6 +317,67 @@
     INTREQR = 0xDFF01E,
 };
 
+/*
+ * Open bus.
+ *
+ * A guest access that reaches the fall-through in SYSWriteValToAddr() or
+ * SYSReadValFromAddr() is emulated by touching the linear alias of the guest
+ * address space at 0xffffff9000000000. When the guest address is one the
+ * machine does not have -- because a pointer went wild, or because software is
+ * sizing memory -- that alias is not mapped, the access faults *inside the
+ * fault handler*, and the handler re-enters itself. 512 KB of ARM stack is gone
+ * in a few thousand turns and the machine hangs with a PC parked on the vector.
+ *
+ * A real 68k board answers an unclaimed address with open bus, or raises a bus
+ * error the guest can trap. Either is better than destroying the emulator.
+ *
+ * Whether an address is backed is asked of the MMU rather than of a table of
+ * regions. `at s1e1r`/`s1e1w` runs the address through the same stage-1
+ * translation the load or store would use and reports the result in PAR_EL1,
+ * bit 0 set meaning it faulted. That cannot disagree with reality, which a
+ * hand-maintained region list can and did: an earlier attempt at this guard
+ * rejected everything outside `sys_memory` above 16 MB, which included the
+ * framebuffer at 0x3c100000 -- the machine booted perfectly and nothing could
+ * ever appear on screen.
+ */
+static inline int alias_readable(uint64_t addr)
+{
+    uint64_t par;
+    __asm__ volatile("at s1e1r, %1; isb; mrs %0, PAR_EL1"
+                     : "=r"(par) : "r"(addr) : "memory");
+    return (par & 1) == 0;
+}
+
+static inline int alias_writable(uint64_t addr)
+{
+    uint64_t par;
+    __asm__ volatile("at s1e1w, %1; isb; mrs %0, PAR_EL1"
+                     : "=r"(par) : "r"(addr) : "memory");
+    return (par & 1) == 0;
+}
+
+/*
+ * Report, but do not drown. A guest looping over unmapped addresses -- memory
+ * sizing does exactly that -- would otherwise turn the serial console into the
+ * new bottleneck. The first few carry the information; the count is what says
+ * whether it was one wild pointer or a sweep.
+ */
+static void open_bus_report(const char *what, uint64_t far, int size, uint64_t value)
+{
+    static uint32_t seen = 0;
+
+    seen++;
+    if (seen <= 8)
+    {
+        kprintf("[JIT:SYS] open bus %s: guest %p size %d value %p (#%d)\n",
+                what, far, size, value, seen);
+    }
+    else if (seen == 9)
+    {
+        kprintf("[JIT:SYS] open bus: further reports suppressed\n");
+    }
+}
+
 #ifdef PISTORM_ANY_MODEL
 
 #include "ps_protocol.h"
@@ -765,6 +826,12 @@
         return 1;
     }
 
+    if (!alias_writable(far + 0xffffff9000000000))
+    {
+        open_bus_report("write", far, size, value);
+        return 1;
+    }
+
     switch(size)
     {
         case 1:
@@ -830,6 +897,16 @@
         return 1;
     }
 
+    if (!alias_readable(far + 0xffffff9000000000))
+    {
+        open_bus_report("read", far, size, 0);
+        /* Open bus floats high on a 68k board with nothing driving it. */
+        *value = ~(uint64_t)0;
+        if (size == 16)
+            *value2 = ~(uint64_t)0;
+        return 1;
+    }
+
     switch(size)
     {
         case 1:
